WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.400 align:start position:19%
At 10 years old, 1 year is 10% of your
entire life. At 40, it's 2.5%.

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:14.880 align:start position:19%
Same year, four times smaller. You
clicked because time really does feel

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:18.760 align:start position:19%
faster every year. And the feeling is
real. But it's not the clock that

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:22.520 align:start position:19%
changed. It's your brain's way of
counting time.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:26.280 align:start position:19%
Here's how it actually works. The first
piece is simple math. Your brain

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:31.760 align:start position:19%
measures a year against everything you
have already lived. 1 year at 10 is a

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:37.120 align:start position:19%
tenth of your whole existence. At 40, it
is 1/40. The same 12 months taking up

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:42.160 align:start position:19%
four times less space in your life. But
math alone is not the whole story. Here

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:46.600 align:start position:19%
is the deeper mechanism. Your brain does
not track time with a clock. It tracks

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:50.560 align:start position:19%
time with memories. Think about your
morning commute. You have done it a

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:55.160 align:start position:19%
thousand times, so your brain barely
records it. A whole hour can vanish from

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:59.040 align:start position:19%
your memory without a trace.
Look back at last week. If you can

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:04.040 align:start position:19%
barely remember it, your brain files it
as almost no time passed. Scientists

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.120 align:start position:19%
call this the holiday paradox. A week of
vacation feels slow while it happens

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:13.360 align:start position:19%
because everything is new. But looking
back, it feels huge and full. A month of

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.480 align:start position:19%
routine, it flies past and leaves almost
nothing behind. Think about it. Can you

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.520 align:start position:19%
name a single Tuesday from 3 months ago?
Probably not. But that one weekend trip

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:26.440 align:start position:19%
to the beach, you remember the smell of
the sunscreen. That is your brain's

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:31.640 align:start position:19%
clock, and it only counts what stands
out. So here's the real formula. New

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:36.240 align:start position:19%
experiences pack your memory fuller, and
your brain reads a fuller memory as a

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:40.600 align:start position:19%
longer stretch of time. Researchers who
study time perception keep finding the

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:45.800 align:start position:19%
same pattern. People who filled their
days with unfamiliar places, faces, and

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:50.960 align:start position:19%
problems remembered those days as much
longer. Routine, on the other hand,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:55.440 align:start position:19%
compresses time like a vacuum. And this
is why childhood felt so long. Every

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:59.880 align:start position:19%
playground was a new country. Every
school year rewrote our whole world.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:04.160 align:start position:19%
Your memory was bursting at the seams,
so your brain honestly believed a single

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:08.360 align:start position:19%
summer could last forever. And here is
the good part. You cannot slow the clock

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:12.200 align:start position:19%
down, but you can slow your brain's
clock. Break the routine, take a

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:17.480 align:start position:19%
different route, try one new thing a
week, learn something tiny. Start small.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:21.800 align:start position:19%
Cook a meal you have never cooked. Call
an old friend you have not heard from in

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:27.280 align:start position:19%
years. Every new memory is a deposit in
your brain's time bank. New footage in,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:31.160 align:start position:19%
more time out. Time is not speeding up.
You are just feeding your brain the same

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:35.360 align:start position:19%
day on repeat. Change the footage and
the years start stretching again.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:40.360 align:start position:19%
If this one rewired how you see time,
subscribe. Ink time draws a new one

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.360 align:start position:19%
every day.

